<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="host-install"
      toc={[
        {"install", "The install command"},
        {"flags", "Flags and environment"},
        {"what-it-does", "What the installer does"},
        {"config", "The config file"},
        {"user", "The runner's user and privileged actions"},
        {"next", "After it's connected"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="host-install">
        <:dek>
          The
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
            quickstart
          </.link>
          gets one runner online in a minute. This page is the full install: every flag, what the
          installer actually does to the host, the service it creates, the config file it writes,
          and the unprivileged user it runs as. For a container instead of a host, see <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/containers"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >Containers</.link>.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_h2 id="install">The install command</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        One command, run as root, on a Linux host with systemd. It downloads the signed binary,
        checksum-verifies it, creates the service user and unit, writes the config, and starts the
        runner — which enrolls with the key and connects. The
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Runners → Connect a runner</strong>
        button generates this exact line with a fresh single-use key baked in.
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="curl -sSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev bash"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -sSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh \
    | sudo EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… EMISAR_URL=https://emisar.dev bash</.docs_code>
      <.docs_verify_download tarball="emisar-<version>-linux-amd64.tar.gz" checksums="SHA256SUMS" />
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        It's a plain, readable shell script —
        <.link href={~p"/install.sh"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">read it first</.link>
        if you'd rather. The host needs outbound 443 to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar.dev</code>, and to
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">github.com</code>
        + <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">api.github.com</code>
        to fetch and verify the release — no inbound rule ever.
      </p>

      <.terminal_cast
        id="host-install-cast"
        label="web-01"
        caption="What that one command does — download, verify, install the hardened service, connect. Nothing listens inbound."
        lines={[
          %{
            k: "cmd",
            p: "$",
            t:
              "curl -sSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-7f3a… bash"
          },
          %{k: "install", t: "install target: linux/amd64 via systemd"},
          %{k: "install", t: "latest release: runner-v0.3.1"},
          %{k: "install", t: "downloading emisar-0.3.1-linux-amd64.tar.gz"},
          %{k: "install", t: "checksum verified  sha256:9f2c8e1a4b6d3f0c…"},
          %{k: "install", t: "creating system user emisar"},
          %{k: "install", t: "writing /etc/emisar/config.yaml + runner.env (mode 0600)"},
          %{k: "install", t: "installed binary to /usr/local/bin/emisar"},
          %{k: "install", t: "installed pack linux-core · debugging · systemd-deep"},
          %{k: "install", t: "writing /etc/systemd/system/emisar.service"},
          %{k: "install", t: "starting emisar.service"},
          %{k: "out", t: "level=INFO msg=cloud.connected actions=89 packs=4 inflight_runs=0"},
          %{
            k: "ok",
            t: "✓ web-01 online — the runner dialed out over WSS; no inbound port opened"
          }
        ]}
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="flags">Flags and environment</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Pass flags after <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">bash -s --</code>;
        pass values as environment variables before <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">bash</code>. The ones you'll reach for:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-900">
        <table class="w-full min-w-[560px] border-collapse text-left text-sm">
          <thead>
            <tr class="border-b border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/80 text-[11px] uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-500">
              <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">Flag / variable</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-2.5 font-medium">What it does</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-400">
            <%= for {name, meaning} <- [
              {"EMISAR_URL", "Your control-plane URL — baked into config.yaml (default https://emisar.dev)."},
              {"EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY", "The emkey-enroll-… key the runner trades for its own token on first connect."},
              {"RUNNER_GROUP / RUNNER_ROLE / RUNNER_ENVIRONMENT", "Seed the runner's group and labels at install time."},
              {"--packs LIST", "Install these packs during setup, e.g. --packs linux-core,docker."},
              {"--version TAG", "Pin a specific release instead of latest."},
              {"--no-start", "Install everything but don't start the service yet — for image bakes."},
              {"--reset-identity", "With a new key on an existing install, drop the cached token and re-enroll as a fresh runner."},
              {"--no-service", "Binary only: skip the service user, unit, and activation. For containers and CI — see Containers."}
            ] do %>
              <tr>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top font-mono text-xs text-brand-300">{name}</td>
                <td class="px-4 py-3 align-top leading-6">{meaning}</td>
              </tr>
            <% end %>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="what-it-does">What the installer does</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Linux + systemd (the production target).</strong>
            Creates an unprivileged
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar</code>
            system user, drops the binary in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/usr/local/bin/emisar</code>, and
            installs a hardened
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar.service</code>
            unit that runs as that user with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">Restart=on-failure</code>. systemd
            is required — the installer checks for it and stops if it's missing, pointing you at
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">--no-service</code>
            for hosts you supervise yourself.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">macOS + launchd (dev / eval).</strong>
            Installs the binary in
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/usr/local/bin</code>
            and a <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">LaunchDaemon</code>
            at
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/Library/LaunchDaemons/com.emisar.runner.plist</code>
            with <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">KeepAlive</code>. Good for
            trying packs on a laptop; production runs on Linux.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Config + secrets.</strong>
            Writes
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>
            (the non-secret settings) and
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/runner.env</code>
            (mode 0600 — the enrollment key and any pack credentials). State and logs live under
            <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/var/lib/emisar</code>
            and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/var/log/emisar</code>.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <.docs_h2 id="config">The config file</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/etc/emisar/config.yaml</code>
        is operator-owned and readable — nothing secret lives here (the key rides in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">runner.env</code>). The fields you'll
        actually set:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="/etc/emisar/config.yaml"><span class="text-sky-300">schema_version</span>: <span class="text-amber-300">1</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">runner</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">group</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"web"</span>              <span class="text-zinc-600"># the fleet tier this host belongs to (targeted by runbooks + scopes)</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">labels</span>:                    <span class="text-zinc-600"># free-form, for finer targeting</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">role</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"frontend"</span>
    <span class="text-sky-300">region</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"us-east-1"</span>
  <span class="text-zinc-600"># id: "web-01"           # optional — defaults to a persisted UUID</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">cloud</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">url</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"https://emisar.dev"</span>
  <span class="text-sky-300">enrollment_key_env</span>: <span class="text-brand-300">"EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY"</span>
<span class="text-sky-300">execution</span>:
  <span class="text-sky-300">inherit_env</span>:              <span class="text-zinc-600"># env-var names a pack may read for credentials</span>
    - <span class="text-brand-300">NOMAD_ADDR</span>
    - <span class="text-brand-300">NOMAD_TOKEN</span>
<span class="text-zinc-600"># admission:               # optional local defense-in-depth — see the security model</span>
<span class="text-zinc-600"># signing:                 # optional signed-dispatch enforcement — see signed dispatch</span></.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Restart the service after editing (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo systemctl restart emisar</code>,
        or SIGHUP to reload packs without dropping runs). Two fields have their own pages:
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">admission</code>
        </.link>
        (a local allow/deny list and risk ceiling the cloud can't override) and
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/signed-dispatch"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">signing</code>
        </.link>
        (require a customer signature on every dispatch).
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="user">The runner's user and privileged actions</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The service runs as the unprivileged
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar</code>
        user, not root — so by default an action can read logs, list processes, and query services,
        but can't touch anything that needs privilege. When a pack action legitimately needs root —
        restarting a system service, editing system config — you grant that one capability
        explicitly, so the blast radius is a list you wrote, not "the runner is root":
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="/etc/sudoers.d/emisar"><span class="text-zinc-500"># let the runner restart exactly nginx, nothing else</span>
emisar ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl restart nginx</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The action calls <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sudo systemctl restart nginx</code>;
        a polkit rule works the same way. On Linux, an action with a
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">user:</code>
        set also drops to that user before exec, so even a runner under a broad account runs the
        declared action as the lower-privilege user. The full host defense-in-depth picture —
        setuid drop, local admission, systemd hardening — is in the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/security-model"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >
          security model
        </.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="next">After it's connected</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Everything past the first connect — groups and labels, pack credentials, updating the binary
        and packs, reconnect behavior, the host-side CLI, and clean removal — lives on the
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/runners"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          Runner fleet
        </.link>
        page. To boot ephemeral hosts from an autoscaling group with one reusable key, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/autoscaling-fleets"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >
          autoscaling fleets
        </.link>; to roll out across a fleet in phases, see the <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/deployment"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >
          production rollout
        </.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
